{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfs1Em3kfF9SJucAECSp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: the full (Dq, B) fit can't be done reliably, so I'm not reporting values for Dq, B or C.**\n\n- High-spin d⁴ in Oh has only one spin-allowed d–d transition, ⁵Eg → ⁵T₂g, whose energy is Δo = 10Dq. All the other excited states are spin-triplet or lower-spin states, so those bands are spin-forbidden and very weak.\n- Cr(II) is a strong Jahn–Teller ion because its ⁵Eg ground state is degenerate. The ⁵Eg ground state and the ⁵T₂g excited state both split. The single asymmetric band (maximum at 14 260 cm⁻¹, shoulder at about 10 970 cm⁻¹) is the Jahn–Teller-split envelope of that one transition. The shoulder is not a second ligand-field transition.\n- A fit in the Tanabe–Sugano model would need two or more independent spin-allowed bands from an undistorted octahedron. Here the data give one transition. It is distorted, and its true centre is not clearly resolved. The Racah B therefore can't be determined, because this transition doesn't depend on B. The spin-forbidden bands that would fix B were not observed.\n- Dq can only be estimated roughly. Δo is about 1.4 × 10⁴ cm⁻¹, so Dq is roughly 1400 cm⁻¹. The Jahn–Teller splitting of about 3300 cm⁻¹ between the two features makes any single value uncertain by several hundred cm⁻¹. I'm not giving a precise Dq.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"High-spin d4 gives one Jahn-Teller-split spin-allowed band (5Eg→5T2g) that is independent of B, so Dq is only roughly estimable and B is undeterminable.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":212,"cache_read_input_tokens":7806,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":212,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global